+## Subtopics
+
+### Q-criterion Isosurface Extraction
+A technique to show vortex structures in a 3D view as a Q-criterion isosurface [S2, unofficial]. It is listed in the 3D spec's Out of scope section as a follow-up that needs its own spec [S2, unofficial]. FluidX3D skips compaction: each cell runs marching cubes on its $2\times2\times2$ Q stencil and emits its triangles directly [S2, unofficial]. Tiling the velocities in local memory cuts the loads per cell from 32 to 2.6 [S2, unofficial]. The WebGPU route computes Q into a scalar buffer once, then emits triangles with `atomicAdd` into a buffer drawn with `drawIndirect`, and binds it read-only in the vertex stage (noting that read-write storage is invisible to vertex shaders) [S2, unofficial]. The render cost competes with the solver for bandwidth, so the steps/s floor ($\ge 480$ at defaults on the M5) still has to hold [S2, unofficial]. Classic WebGPU marching cubes (mark, scan, compact, emit) takes 40.8–54.4 ms on an M2 Max for $256^3$ resolution [S2, unofficial]. When scaled to 2M cells, this takes roughly 5–7 ms, which is considered too much to spend every frame [S2, unofficial].
